;; The ACPI table checksum: do these `length` bytes sum to zero, mod 256?
;;
;; `checksum_ok` in kernel/acpi.c was the whole of it --
;;
;;   static int checksum_ok(const void *table, uint32_t length) {
;;     const uint8_t *bytes = table; uint8_t sum = 0;
;;     for (uint32_t i = 0; i < length; i++) sum = (uint8_t)(sum + bytes[i]);
;;     return sum == 0;
;;   }
;;
;; -- and it is the only thing standing between firmware's claim about a table
;; and the kernel believing it. acpi.c was the largest file in the kernel with
;; no decision moved out of it at all: every checksum and every bound was still
;; C, over bytes the kernel did not write and cannot re-derive. That is the
;; whole argument for moving it. The page tables at least describe memory this
;; kernel allocated; an ACPI table describes memory somebody else filled in.
;;
;; Four call sites use it and they do not agree on a length: the RSDP at a fixed
;; 20, the ACPI 2.0 extended RSDP at its own declared `length`, and every SDT
;; (XSDT/RSDT, MADT, DMAR) at the length in its header. All four now arrive
;; here, so all four get the same arithmetic and the same bound.
;;
;; THE WRAP -- why a wider accumulator is not merely different
;;
;; The C accumulates in a `uint8_t`. The sum wraps at 256 and the test is
;; `sum == 0`, so what is actually being asked is "sum == 0 (mod 256)". An
;; implementation that accumulates in a wide integer and then asks `total == 0`
;; answers a STRICTLY STRONGER question, and every table whose bytes sum to
;; 256, or 512, or 0x100 * anything -- which is every valid ACPI table with at
;; least one non-zero byte, i.e. all of them -- gets rejected. The mirror-image
;; mistake, comparing a wide total to zero after summing a table that is
;; genuinely corrupt, cannot happen; the danger is entirely in the other
;; direction and the failure would be total rather than subtle.
;;
;; This object accumulates in i64 and masks ONCE, at the end:
;;
;;   (= (bit-and total 255) 0)
;;
;; That is exactly the C, not an approximation of it. Addition is a
;; homomorphism onto Z/256: masking after every add and masking after the last
;; add agree for every input, because ((a + b) mod 256) = ((a mod 256) + b) mod
;; 256. Masking once is chosen over masking per step only because it is one
;; operation instead of 16384 of them, and the per-step mask would have to be
;; re-argued every time the step shape changes. The accumulator cannot overflow
;; on the way there: 16384 bytes of 0xff is 4,177,920, which is 41 bits short
;; of where i64 stops being exact.
;;
;; THE BOUND -- 16 KiB, and it is a ceiling this subset imposes, not a choice
;;
;; `kernel-memory-operations` in the compiler frontend offers exactly three
;; widths of bounded byte load: `kernel-load-u8` (512), `kernel-load-u8-4k`
;; (4096) and `kernel-load-u8-16k` (16384). There is no 64 KiB form. 16384 is
;; therefore the largest table this object can be made to walk at all, and the
;; C it replaces admitted up to ACPI_MAX_TABLE_SIZE = 1 MiB. That narrowing is
;; real and is called out in the handoff rather than hidden here.
;;
;; It is not a narrowing that bites anything acpi.c actually parses. The tables
;; that reach this object are the RSDP (20 or 36 bytes), the XSDT/RSDT (36 plus
;; eight bytes per table the firmware publishes -- tens of entries, not
;; thousands), the MADT (44 plus one entry per interrupt controller, and this
;; kernel stops recording at 256 CPUs) and the DMAR (36 plus a DRHD per IOMMU).
;; A 16 KiB MADT is on the order of a thousand x2APIC entries. The DSDT, the one
;; ACPI table that is routinely megabytes, is never touched here -- acpi.c does
;; not walk it.
;;
;; The bound is enforced BEFORE the first load, and that ordering is the whole
;; point. `kernel-load-u8-16k` does not return an error when its declared length
;; exceeds 16384: it reaches `ud2`, and `ud2` in this kernel is a halt, not a
;; rejected table. A firmware-declared length of 100000 has to be turned into a
;; 0 by this object's own guard or it becomes a boot that stops with an
;; unexpected-vector exception.
;;
;; FUEL -- 8 bytes per step, because the tier is 4096 and the walk is 16384
;;
;; `emit-function` charges one fuel unit per function entry, and a tail-self
;; call re-enters through the same charge. A one-byte-per-step walk therefore
;; costs one unit per byte, and this object's registered tier
;; (`bounded-memory?` in kotoba-native's elf64.clj) is 4096: a 4096-byte table
;; would spend 4098 and hit the prologue `ud2`, and a 16 KiB table would be four
;; times over. The tier is not this repository's to change, so the step is.
;;
;; Eight loads per step divides the count by eight and leaves the remainder to
;; single-byte steps, in the SAME function so the whole walk stays tail-self and
;; the native stack does not grow. Measured, not estimated:
;;
;;   16384 bytes -> 2048 eight-byte steps + 1 terminating entry  = 2049
;;   16383 bytes -> 2047 eight-byte steps + 7 single + 1         = 2055
;;   plus one entry for `aiueos-acpi-checksum-ok` itself         =   +1
;;
;; so 2056 units is the worst case over the whole admitted range, against a
;; 4096 replenish -- a factor of two of headroom, which is what stops this from
;; being the size-dependent trap the network checksums nearly were. Four bytes
;; per step would NOT have been enough: 4096 steps + 1 + 1 is 4098.
;;
;; SIGNEDNESS -- the C is unsigned, this object is signed i64
;;
;; `length` reaches the C as a `uint32_t` and this object as an i64, so every
;; value the C could see is non-negative here and the two arithmetics agree on
;; all of them. The guards are still written to be total over i64 rather than
;; over what the current call sites happen to pass:
;;
;;   * `(<= length 0)` rather than `(= length 0)`. A negative length would pass
;;     `(< length 16384)` in signed arithmetic while the C's unsigned form saw a
;;     value near 2^64 and rejected it. The load's own bound check is unsigned
;;     (`cmp rcx,maximum` / `ja`), so a negative length would trap rather than
;;     return -- one clause has to catch it first, and `<= 0` catches the zero
;;     case and the negative case together.
;;   * `(<= table 0)` for the base. A pointer at or above 2^63 arrives negative;
;;     the C never checked the pointer here at all (its callers did, through
;;     `bounded_address`), and a null or negative base reaching the load is a
;;     trap. Guarding it here makes the object total on its own signature
;;     instead of on its callers' discipline.
;;
;; LENGTH 0 -- a deliberate divergence, fail-closed
;;
;; The C returns 1 for a zero length: the loop does not execute, `sum` stays 0,
;; and 0 == 0. That is vacuously true and was harmless only because every one of
;; the four call sites had already established a length of at least 20. This
;; object returns 0 instead. A checksum over no bytes is not a checksum that
;; passed, and an object that answers "verified" to a caller that has verified
;; nothing is the wrong thing to leave lying around in a kernel. The divergence
;; is unreachable from acpi.c as it stands today; it exists so that it stays
;; unreachable from whatever calls this next.

(defn aiueos-acpi-checksum-step [table length offset total]
  ;; Three arms: done, fewer than eight bytes left, a full eight. The
  ;; eight-load arm and the one-load arm are both tail-self calls into this
  ;; same function, so the remainder costs a branch rather than a second
  ;; function (which would have cost a fuel unit per remaining byte on top of
  ;; the step's own).
  ;;
  ;; Every load names `table` as its region and adds the offset inside the
  ;; index, because the compiler rejects a computed base outright -- "kernel
  ;; memory base must name a region, not compute one". `length` is the region
  ;; length, so the load's own index check is against the table's real end
  ;; rather than against a constant 16384 the caller may not own.
  (if (>= offset length)
    total
    (if (> (+ offset 8) length)
      (aiueos-acpi-checksum-step table length (+ offset 1)
        (+ total (kernel-load-u8-16k table length offset)))
      (aiueos-acpi-checksum-step table length (+ offset 8)
        (+ total
           (+ (+ (+ (kernel-load-u8-16k table length offset)
                    (kernel-load-u8-16k table length (+ offset 1)))
                 (+ (kernel-load-u8-16k table length (+ offset 2))
                    (kernel-load-u8-16k table length (+ offset 3))))
              (+ (+ (kernel-load-u8-16k table length (+ offset 4))
                    (kernel-load-u8-16k table length (+ offset 5)))
                 (+ (kernel-load-u8-16k table length (+ offset 6))
                    (kernel-load-u8-16k table length (+ offset 7))))))))))

(defn aiueos-acpi-checksum-ok [table length]
  ;; `and` and `or` desugar to nested `let`/`if` and short-circuit, so the walk
  ;; below is not reached -- and no load is issued -- unless every guard has
  ;; already held.
  ;; The verdict is spelled as a nested `if` rather than as the comparison's
  ;; own result because `if` branches must agree on a value type, and a literal
  ;; 0 is an i64 where a comparison is a bool.
  (if (or (<= table 0) (<= length 0) (> length 16384))
    0
    (if (= (bit-and (aiueos-acpi-checksum-step table length 0 0) 255) 0)
      1
      0)))

(defn main [] 0)
